{# Bundle base: the SHELL's page frame plus the sheets the Files hub needs.

   Every screen of this bundle extends this rather than the shell directly, so
   the hub's vocabulary loads only where the hub renders and the preview overlay
   is included exactly once per page.

   THE FRAME IS THE SHELL'S (ShellBundle, in the core), not this module's. The
   trail, the page head and the flashes are the frame's sockets, and a module
   that types its own copies is a module that drifts from every other screen in
   the installation.

   THE SHELL'S WIDGET SHEET IS NOT OPTIONAL. The hub IS a widget dashboard, so
   `.w-grid`, `.w-cell`, `.w-span-*` and the whole library chrome are drawn by
   the shell's widget stylesheet, which its own header says must be linked by the
   page rendering it. Without it the grid has nothing declaring a span and every
   widget renders one column wide.

   Ordered shell → widget → preview → hub, weakest to strongest: this module's
   own sheets are the ones allowed to override, and they are last.

   Symfony docs — https://symfony.com/doc/current/frontend/asset_mapper.html :
   "All bundles that have a Resources/public/ or public/ directory will
   automatically have that directory added as an asset path, using the namespace:
   bundles/<BundleName>" — and under AssetMapper those assets are automatically
   content-versioned. So public/files.css is served as
   bundles/uhifadhistorage/files.css with a digest, no assets:install. #}
{% extends '@Shell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Bundle\\ShellBundle\\ShellBundle::WIDGET_STYLESHEET')) }}">
    {# The PREVIEW's vocabulary next: it belongs to the shareable component, not
       to the hub, and another module's page loads exactly this one file without
       files.css. The hub is one of its consumers, so it loads it the same way. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('bundles/uhifadhistorage/preview.css') }}">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Storage\\UhifadhiStorageBundle::STYLESHEET')) }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block javascripts %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {# The hub's OWN behaviour: the removal form, and nothing else. Plain DOM —
       it runs on the file page only and nothing outside this bundle asks for it.
       The filter row's dropdowns and the preview are Stimulus controllers
       instead: both are included into surfaces this file does not render, so
       each has to arm itself wherever it lands. #}
    <script src="{{ asset('bundles/uhifadhistorage/files.js') }}" defer></script>
{% endblock %}

{# THE TAB SOCKET IS THE FRAME'S, AND IT IS FILLED. The section wears the area
   idiom: its four screens are a strip in the same position an area's are, and
   the frame resolves that strip from the surface marker each of the four
   routes carries — Overview · Files · Sources · Storage, with Configure the
   action at the right-hand end of the header rather than a tab.

   A SCREEN THAT IS NOT ONE OF THE FOUR BLANKS IT ITSELF. A file's own page and
   the widget library are inside the section without being screens of it; they
   carry no marker, so the frame would fall back to an AREA's strip, and a
   strip naming an area would be the page claiming a place it is not in. Each
   of those two templates fills this socket with nothing. #}

{% block shell_page %}
    {% block storage_page %}{% endblock %}
    {# The preview, once per page, for every tile and row drawn above it. #}
    {{ include('@UhifadhiStorage/overlay/_preview.html.twig') }}
{% endblock %}
